  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 114 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nGuidance continues to suggest that the train of shortwaves \nundercutting the ridging across the eastern CONUS will see its next \npush on Thursday night, with rain chances progressively invading \nfurther south by Friday evening. Daytime rain chances on Friday \nremain fairly low at this point due to the ensemble predictability \nlimit, with 15-20% PoPs staying west of I-49 during the day, but as \nhigher-resolution regional guidance begins to get into range through \nthe week, these PoPs will likely become more refined spatially and \ntemporally as mesoscale influences and the location of the \nshortwaves become better resolved.\n\nGlobal ensembles and deterministic models remain well in agreement \nthat the eventual breakdown of the ridge and widespread precip \nchances will be here by Friday night and Saturday, with an upper-\nlevel low lifting into the Southern Plains overnight Friday into \nSaturday. As a result, broad-brushed 35-50% rain chances on Saturday \n(Halloween)currently spread across the entire forecast area. It is \npossible at this point that Saturday does not remain a complete \nwashout depending on the finer-scale details that are not yet \nresolved by longer range models, but because of the lack of \ncertainty in placement, timing, and intensity of the wave due to how \nfar out in the forecast period Saturday still is, we don't have many \nother details to report for the trick-or-treat forecast at this \ntime, so stay tuned through the week as details become more clear. \n\nAs far as temperatures go, cloud cover associated with the \nshortwaves/rainfall will have the potential to limit temperatures \nsome over the weekend. Signals don't currently suggest there will be \nsignificant air mass modification (aka, a cold frontal passage) as \nthe shortwave moves through Friday night/Saturday, so cloud cover \nand rainfall will be the primary factor to monitor as far as \ntemperatures go this weekend, otherwise remaining relatively steady. \nHowever, this also means there won't be a major drop in \ntemperatures, so current guidance suggesting highs between 65-75 \ndegrees seems like a fairly safe bet, even at such an extended time \nrange.",
